    <section id="pod" class="article-section article-section-wide">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.1 · the complete deployment shape</span>
        <h2>Everything with authority is visible in the pod.</h2>
        <p>There is no Service, Ingress, HTTP health endpoint, or TCP listener. The only internal endpoint is a <code>0600</code> socket in a memory-backed shared volume.</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="kubernetes-cutaway">
        <div class="k8s-cutaway-title"><span>Kubernetes Deployment · replicas: 1 · strategy: Recreate</span><code>runAsUser: 65532</code></div>
        <div class="k8s-init-row">
          <article><small>init container · root + CHOWN/FOWNER only</small><strong>prepare-files</strong><span>projected inputs → real 0600 files owned by 65532</span></article>
          <i aria-hidden="true">finishes before daemons start ↓</i>
        </div>
        <div class="k8s-runtime-row">
          <article class="k8s-gateway-box"><small>regular container</small><strong>dekopond</strong><ul><li>Slack / Telegram</li><li>model client</li><li>bounded sessions</li><li>no provider credential</li></ul></article>
          <div class="k8s-socket-box"><span>memory-backed emptyDir</span><strong>broker.sock</strong><code>0600 · UID 65532</code><i aria-hidden="true">proposal →<br>← result</i></div>
          <article class="k8s-broker-box"><small>native sidecar</small><strong>dekopon-brokerd</strong><ul><li>Cedar policy</li><li>constraint sets</li><li>provider credentials</li><li>Wasm + audit</li></ul></article>
        </div>
        <div class="k8s-volume-row">
          <div><small>memory</small><strong>/etc/dekopon</strong><span>config · policy · provider credentials</span></div>
          <div><small>image</small><strong>/opt/dekopon/providers</strong><span>owner-matched Wasm components</span></div>
          <div><small>persistent claim</small><strong>/var/lib/dekopon</strong><span>audit · checkpoint · ChatGPT credential directory</span></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>One image supplies both commands. Separate containers preserve process responsibilities; the shared UID and filesystem namespace satisfy the current authenticated local transport.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="one-pod" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.2 · topology follows transport</span>
        <h2>The Unix socket decides the placement.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Constraint</th><th scope="col">Kubernetes consequence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Broker binds a filesystem Unix socket</th><td>Gateway and broker need one shared pod volume.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Socket mode is <code>0600</code></th><td>Both processes run as UID/GID <code>65532:65532</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Peer identity uses <code>SO_PEERCRED</code></th><td>A Service or TCP proxy would discard the authenticated transport.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Audit has one exclusive writer</th><td>The Deployment is fixed at one replica with <code>Recreate</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Gateway probes the broker once</th><td>The broker runs as a native sidecar whose startup probe gates gateway start.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Two images would still need the same version and pod. Version 0.4.0 therefore ships one image containing all four executables. Each container selects its command and retains its own responsibility.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="files" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.3 · fail-closed file hygiene</span>
        <h2>A Kubernetes Secret mount is not a broker credential file.</h2>
        <p>Projected ConfigMap and Secret volumes are symlink farms owned by root. The broker deliberately refuses symlinks, wrong owners, multiple links, and unsafe mode bits.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="file-materialization">
        <div><small>Kubernetes projection</small><strong><code>key → ..data/key</code></strong><span>root-owned symlink · rejected</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">init container copies →</i>
        <div><small>private emptyDir</small><strong>regular file</strong><span>one link · UID 65532 · mode 0600</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">daemon opens →</i>
        <div><small>startup validation</small><strong>serve or fail closed</strong><span>never “warn and continue”</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mounted shape</th><th scope="col">Why the broker refuses it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Secret / ConfigMap volume</th><td><code>O_NOFOLLOW</code> stops at the projection symlink.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>subPath</code></th><td>The file is real but root-owned and normally <code>0644</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>fsGroup</code></th><td>Group readability is exactly what the credentials file rejects.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Init-container copy</th><td>Produces a fresh owner-only regular file that passes every check.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The projected source is visible only to <code>prepare-files</code>. The two daemons mount the materialized output, not the Kubernetes projection.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="state" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.4 · durable authority history</span>
        <h2>Audit and checkpoint move together or not at all.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="audit-volume-map">
        <div><span>append first</span><strong>audit.jsonl</strong><small>hash-linked records · synchronized</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">then →</i>
        <div><span>replace atomically</span><strong>audit-checkpoint.json</strong><small>record count + chain head</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">guarded by →</i>
        <div><span>single writer</span><strong>checkpoint lock</strong><small>same persistent directory</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>All three paths live on one persistent claim because checkpoint replacement stages a sibling file and renames it atomically. The claim carries a keep-on-uninstall annotation. Deleting one file to bypass recovery makes startup fail; deleting both together is a rollback local state cannot detect.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Size the volume before creating it.</strong>
        <p>The target single-node storage class cannot expand. The chart defaults to 2 GiB and lowers the per-record ceiling so the broker’s designed record cap binds before the filesystem fills.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="startup" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.5 · ordering without fake HTTP health</span>
        <h2>A real broker request is the readiness check.</h2>
      </div>

      <ol class="mental-model-list compact-steps">
        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Materialize files.</strong><p>The init container copies, owns, modes, and verifies each required file.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Compile providers and restore audit.</strong><p>The broker validates configuration, Cedar, credentials, components, audit, and checkpoint before binding.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Answer a real capability query.</strong><p>The startup probe uses <code>dekopon-run broker capabilities</code> across the real socket and peer checks.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Start the gateway.</strong><p>Kubernetes starts the regular container only after the native broker sidecar passes startup.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Drain in reverse.</strong><p>Gateway sessions stop first. The broker then finishes in-flight effects and synchronizes audit state.</p></div></li>
      </ol>

      <p>There is no liveness probe. An automatic restart could kill a broker mid-invocation and would not repair an external dependency. Readiness reports truth without manufacturing that risk.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="security" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>K.6 · pod security posture</span>
        <h2>Root exists for one bounded setup job.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Daemons</span></div><h3>Non-root and read-only</h3><p>UID 65532, no added capabilities, no privilege escalation, read-only root filesystems, and RuntimeDefault seccomp.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Init</span></div><h3>Root with two capabilities</h3><p><code>CHOWN</code> and <code>FOWNER</code> create the ownership the daemons insist on. Everything else is dropped.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-direction"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Current limit</span></div><h3>One shared UID</h3><p>This provides attribution and deny-by-default scoping, not gateway isolation from the broker trust domain.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>Wasmtime JIT compilation requires executable memory operations allowed by RuntimeDefault seccomp. A hand-written tighter profile must prove a real component still loads.</p>
    </section>

